    <hasEvidenceChunk rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">2306.00014_rag::chunk_003</hasEvidenceChunk>
    <hasEvidenceChunk rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">2303.08302_rag::chunk_000</hasEvidenceChunk>
    <hasEvidenceChunk rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">2103.13630_rag::chunk_014</hasEvidenceChunk>
    <hasEvidenceChunk rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">136710726_rag::chunk_002</hasEvidenceChunk>
    <hasEvidenceChunk rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">136720190_rag::chunk_001</hasEvidenceChunk>
    <hasEvidenceChunk rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">2302.04304_rag::chunk_001</hasEvidenceChunk>
    <hasEvidenceChunk rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">2103.13630_rag::chunk_012</hasEvidenceChunk>
    <hasEvidenceChunk rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">2201.08442_rag::chunk_025</hasEvidenceChunk>
    <hasEvidenceChunk rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">2311.12359_rag::chunk_001</hasEvidenceChunk>
    <hasEvidenceChunk rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">2303.08302_rag::chunk_001</hasEvidenceChunk>
    <hasEvidenceChunk rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">2304.09785_rag::chunk_003</hasEvidenceChunk>
    <hasEvidenceChunk rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">2103.01049_rag::chunk_003</hasEvidenceChunk>
    <hasEvidenceChunk rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">2402.18158_rag::chunk_000</hasEvidenceChunk>
    <hasEvidenceChunk rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Lin_Bit-Shrinking_Limiting_Instantaneous_Sharpness_for_Improving_Post-Training_Quantization_CVPR_2023_paper_rag::chunk_001</hasEvidenceChunk>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Existing quantization methods can be categorized into two prominent branches: quantization-aware training and post-training quantization.</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Post-training quantization (PTQ) has emerged as a promising technique for mitigating memory consumption and computational costs in large language models (LLMs).</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">In PTQ, all the weights and activations quantization parameters are determined without any re-training of the NN model.</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">According to the criterion of whether training the quantized network or not, the quantization methods could be roughly divided into two categories: Post Training Quantization (PTQ)</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">PTQ quantizes the network with unlabeled calibration images</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">post-training quantization (PTQ) on the diffusion model. PTQ has already been well studied in other learning domains like classification and object detection</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">done without re-training, a process that is often referred to as Post-Training Quantization (PTQ).</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Post-training quantization techniques take a pre-trained FP32 networks and convert it into a xedpoint network without the need for the original training pipeline.</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">In PTQ, all the weights and activations quantization parameters are determined without any re-training of the NN model. As such, PTQ is a very fast method for quantizing NN models.</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">post-training quantization (PTQ) [1][3] has been the prevailing choice for model compression, offering high model accuracy with reduced compute and memory usage.</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">extant literature falls short in providing thorough coverage of the functionality of various PTQ methods</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">PTQ searches for the optimal quantization parameters by solving the problem of minimizing the local reconstruction loss</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">post-training quantization that adapts to the data and the task loss. However, these aforementioned methods also require access to limited data for recovering the performance.</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Post-training quantization (PTQ) has emerged as a promising technique to reduce the cost of large language models (LLMs). Specifically, PTQ can effectively mitigate memory consumption</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">PTQ ef- ciently turns a pre-trained full precision network to quantized one with only a small calibration set.</hasEvidenceText>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/S0/R1#enablesattackon">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#ObjectProperty"/>
    <rdfs:domain rdf:resource="http://example.org/S0/R1#model"/>
    <rdfs:range rdf:resource="http://example.org/S0/R1#model"/>
    <rdfs:label rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">enablesAttackOn</rdfs:label>
    <skos:skos_definition rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Quantization can be used to weaponize perturbations for injecting undesirable behaviors into a model.</skos:skos_definition>
    <hasCQ rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">CQ_S0_R1_0138</hasCQ>
    <prov:wasDerivedFrom rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">CQ_S0_R1_0138</prov:wasDerivedFrom>
    <hasEvidenceChunk rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">0kCxbBQknN_rag::chunk_003</hasEvidenceChunk>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">weaponize quantization perturbations for injecting undesirable behaviors.</hasEvidenceText>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/S0/R1#Axiom_55">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#NamedIndividual"/>
